    "text": "[from] Roger Oberholtzer <drtyu@opq.se>\n[body] Re: [opensuse] NFS, openSUSE 10.0 and 10.3\nOn Fri, 2008-02-15 at 16:05 +0000, Dave Howorth wrote: \n> Roger Oberholtzer wrote:\n> > Hello\n> > \n> > I have encountered an unexpected problem with the nfs client on openSUSE\n> > 10.3. Even as root, I cannot mount an NFS share from a 10.0 system on a\n> > 10.3 client. I have searched the archives and do not see this problem. I\n> > have seen reports of mounts not happening at boot or of non-root users\n> > not being able to mount things. This is not what I am experiencing.\n> > \n> > I can mount between two 10.3 systems. Or between two 10.0 systems. And,\n> > 10.0 can mount from a 10.3 system. Only mounting on a 10.3 client system\n> > from a 10.0 server fails.\n> \n> IIRC, the defaults as regards NFS version were changed for 10.3. The\n> \"2GB File size limit exceeded over NFS on 10.3\" thread from December may\n> give you some clues.\n\nJust what I tried first on the 10.3 client: -onfsvers=2 or -onfsvers=3.\nSame complaint. \n\nlinux:~ # mount -t nfs -onfsvers=3 -otcp 10.1.6.131:/home /tmp/qq\nmount: 10.1.6.131:/home failed, reason given by server: Permission\ndenied\n\nlinux:~ # mount -t nfs -onfsvers=2 -otcp 10.1.6.131:/home /tmp/qq\nmount: 10.1.6.131:/home",
